insert into d (name, yr, txt) values
  ('Battle of Prague', 1757, 'On 6 May 1757 Frederick the Great''s Prussian army defeated an Austrian army outside Prague in a very costly battle of the Seven Years'' War. The Prussians lost about 14,000 men, and Frederick found he was not strong enough to storm the city and had to settle for a siege. The Austrian victory at Kolín a few weeks later forced him to give it up, and the war turned into a long struggle for survival for Prussia.'),
  ('Treaty of Paris ends Seven Years'' War in North America', 1763, 'The Treaty of Paris, signed on 10 February 1763, ended the Seven Years'' War between Britain, France and Spain. France gave up Canada and its lands east of the Mississippi to Britain, and Spain received Louisiana west of the river. Britain was now the leading colonial power, but its huge war debts led it to tax the American colonies, which helped lead to the American Revolution.'),
  ('Boston Massacre', 1770, 'On 5 March 1770 British soldiers guarding the Boston Custom House fired into a hostile crowd, killing five colonists. Radicals such as Samuel Adams and Paul Revere spread the story as an example of British tyranny, and an engraving made it famous across the colonies. At the trial, defended by John Adams, most of the soldiers were acquitted, but the incident hardened opposition to British rule.'),
  ('Boston Tea Party', 1773, 'On 16 December 1773 members of the Sons of Liberty, some disguised as Mohawk Indians, boarded three ships in Boston Harbor and threw 342 chests of British tea into the water. It was a protest against the Tea Act and against taxation without representation. Britain answered with harsh punishments called the Coercive Acts, and the colonies moved closer to open rebellion.'),
  ('Battle of Bunker Hill', 1775, 'On 17 June 1775 British troops drove American militia from the heights above Charlestown, near Boston, but lost more than a thousand men, almost half of those who attacked. The colonists, short of powder, were told by tradition not to fire until they saw the whites of the enemy''s eyes. Though the Americans lost the position, the battle proved that farmers and townsmen could stand up to British regulars.'),
  ('Battle of the Great Bridge', 1775, 'On 9 December 1775 Virginia militia and minutemen defeated British regulars and loyalists at Great Bridge, near Norfolk. The royal governor, Lord Dunmore, left the city and took to British ships, ending British control of Virginia. It was the first Revolutionary War battle in the colony and showed that Virginia had effectively thrown off royal rule.'),
  ('Battle of Long Island', 1776, 'On 27 August 1776 the British under General Howe defeated Washington''s Continental Army in Brooklyn, in the largest battle of the Revolutionary War. Washington''s army was in danger of being trapped but escaped across the East River on the night of 29-30 August, in a daring withdrawal. The British took New York City, which they held for the rest of the war.'),
  ('Declaration of Independence', 1776, 'On 4 July 1776 the Second Continental Congress adopted the Declaration of Independence, mainly drafted by Thomas Jefferson, announcing that the thirteen colonies were free of British rule. It states that all men are created equal and have rights to life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness, and that governments rule by the consent of the governed. The document gave the Revolution its ideals and later inspired independence and rights movements around the world.'),
  ('Battle of Camden', 1780, 'On 16 August 1780 Lord Cornwallis''s smaller British army crushed a larger American army under Horatio Gates at Camden, South Carolina. Many of the American militia fled at the first British bayonet charge, and Gates himself rode away from the field. It was one of the worst American defeats of the war, but it led to Gates''s replacement by the capable Nathanael Greene.'),
  ('Battle of Charleston', 1780, 'On 12 May 1780 the British captured Charleston, South Carolina, and its entire American garrison of about 5,000 men under General Benjamin Lincoln after a siege of six weeks. It was the worst American defeat of the Revolutionary War and the largest surrender of American troops until 1942. It gave the British control of the South for a time, but guerrilla resistance and Greene''s campaign later turned the tide.'),
  ('Battle of Guilford Court House', 1781, 'On 15 March 1781 Cornwallis''s British army defeated Nathanael Greene''s larger American army at Guilford Court House in North Carolina, but lost about a quarter of its men. Cornwallis could not stay in the Carolinas and marched to Virginia, which took him to Yorktown. It is remembered as a pyrrhic victory, and a British politician said that another such victory would ruin the British army.'),
  ('Battle of Yorktown', 1781, 'In September and October 1781 George Washington''s American army and Rochambeau''s French army, with a French fleet blocking the sea, trapped Lord Cornwallis at Yorktown, Virginia. Cornwallis surrendered about 7,000 men on 19 October, and the British band reportedly played ''The World Turned Upside Down''. It was the last major battle of the Revolutionary War and led to British recognition of American independence in 1783.'),
  ('U.S. Constitution signed', 1787, 'On 17 September 1787 delegates at the Constitutional Convention in Philadelphia signed the U.S. Constitution, replacing the weak Articles of Confederation with a stronger federal government. It set up three branches of government with checks and balances, and the states ratified it in 1788. The Bill of Rights was added in 1791, and the Constitution is the oldest written national constitution still in use.'),
  ('Declaration of the Rights of Man', 1789, 'On 26 August 1789 the French National Constituent Assembly adopted the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen, stating that men are born and remain free and equal in rights. It guaranteed liberty, property, security and resistance to oppression, and it made the nation sovereign rather than the king. Inspired by the Enlightenment, it became a model for later declarations of human rights around the world.'),
  ('French Revolution begins', 1789, 'In 1789 France''s financial crisis forced Louis XVI to call the Estates-General, and the Third Estate then declared itself the National Assembly and swore the Tennis Court Oath. Within months the Bastille fell, the feudal privileges of the nobility were abolished, and the Declaration of the Rights of Man was adopted. The Revolution ended the absolute monarchy in France, led to a republic in 1792, and spread ideas of liberty and equality across Europe.'),
  ('Storming of the Bastille', 1789, 'On 14 July 1789 a Paris crowd stormed the Bastille, a royal fortress and prison, seeking gunpowder and weapons and a symbol of royal tyranny. Only seven prisoners were inside, and the governor was killed by the mob. The fall of the Bastille showed that the king could no longer control Paris, and 14 July is now France''s national holiday.'),
  ('Haitian Revolution begins', 1791, 'In August 1791 enslaved people in the French colony of Saint-Domingue, then the richest colony in the world, rose in a great revolt, led at first by Boukman and soon by Toussaint Louverture. Over the next dozen years they defeated French, Spanish and British armies. It was the only slave rebellion in history to found a lasting state, and it shook the slave-owning societies of the Atlantic world.'),
  ('Reign of Terror', 1793, 'During the Reign of Terror of 1793-1794, the revolutionary government under the Committee of Public Safety used mass arrests and the guillotine against its enemies, real or suspected. Tens of thousands of people were executed or died in prison, among them Marie Antoinette. It ended with the fall and execution of its leader Maximilien Robespierre in July 1794, and it remains a warning about revolutionary violence.'),
  ('French abolition of slavery in colonies', 1794, 'On 4 February 1794 the French National Convention abolished slavery in all French colonies, the first great European power to do so. The decree followed the slave uprising in Saint-Domingue, and the Revolution''s ideal of equal rights. Napoleon reinstated slavery in 1802, and France abolished it for good only in 1848.'),
  ('Battle of the Pyramids', 1798, 'On 21 July 1798 Napoleon''s French army defeated the Mamluk cavalry of Egypt near Cairo, in what he named the Battle of the Pyramids. The French infantry formed hollow squares that the Mamluk charges could not break, and he is said to have told his soldiers that forty centuries looked down upon them. The victory gave France control of Egypt for a time, and the scholars who came with the army began the modern study of ancient Egypt.'),
  ('Battle of Heliopolis', 1800, 'On 20 March 1800 the French army in Egypt under General Kléber defeated a much larger Ottoman army near Heliopolis, just outside Cairo, and then put down an uprising in the city. The victory kept Egypt in French hands after Napoleon had left, but Kléber was assassinated in June. Without reinforcements the French were forced out of Egypt by British and Ottoman troops in 1801.'),
  ('Battle of Marengo', 1800, 'On 14 June 1800 Napoleon, the First Consul, won the Battle of Marengo in northern Italy after it had seemed lost. The Austrians under Melas nearly broke the French in the morning, but the arrival of General Desaix, who was killed in the counterattack, turned the day. The victory drove Austria out of Italy and made Napoleon''s political position in Paris secure.'),
  ('Louisiana Purchase', 1803, 'In 1803 the United States bought the huge territory of Louisiana from France for $15 million, doubling the size of the country. Napoleon, who needed money for his wars and had lost his hopes of a New World empire after the defeat in Haiti, was glad to sell. President Thomas Jefferson''s purchase opened the American West to settlement and led to the Lewis and Clark Expedition.'),
  ('Haitian independence', 1804, 'On 1 January 1804 Jean-Jacques Dessalines declared the independence of Haiti, the first Black republic and the only modern state founded by a successful slave revolt. After a brutal war, the former slaves had defeated Napoleon''s army. Many powers refused to recognize Haiti, and France later demanded a huge indemnity, which burdened the country for generations.'),
  ('Napoleon becomes emperor', 1804, 'On 2 December 1804, in Notre-Dame cathedral in Paris, Napoleon Bonaparte crowned himself Emperor of the French in the presence of Pope Pius VII, taking the crown from the pope to put it on his own head. It ended the French Republic and made Napoleon the ruler of France with hereditary rights. His rule brought the Napoleonic Code, which spread legal equality, and a series of wars that reshaped Europe.'),
  ('Battle of Austerlitz', 1805, 'On 2 December 1805 Napoleon crushed a larger Russian and Austrian army at Austerlitz, in what is often called his greatest victory. He pretended to be weak and let the Allies attack his right flank, then struck the weakened centre on the Pratzen Heights. The victory ended the Third Coalition and Austria''s war, and in 1806 the Holy Roman Empire was dissolved.'),
  ('Battle of Jena-Auerstedt', 1806, 'On 14 October 1806 Napoleon destroyed the Prussian army at Jena, while Marshal Davout, with a much smaller force, defeated the main Prussian army at Auerstedt on the same day. Prussia collapsed within weeks and Napoleon entered Berlin. The humiliation led Prussia to carry out far-reaching military and social reforms, which prepared its recovery.'),
  ('Battle of Eylau', 1807, 'On 7-8 February 1807 Napoleon''s army fought the Russians at Eylau in East Prussia, in a snowstorm, in a bloody battle that settled nothing. A huge cavalry charge led by Murat saved the French centre, but casualties were terrible on both sides. It was Napoleon''s first serious check in the field, and a famous painting shows him on the snowy battlefield among the dead.'),
  ('Battle of Friedland', 1807, 'On 14 June 1807 Napoleon defeated the Russian army of General Bennigsen at Friedland, in East Prussia, with the Russians trapped with a river at their backs. The Russian army was routed with heavy losses. It forced Tsar Alexander I to make peace at Tilsit, which left Napoleon master of most of the European continent.'),
  ('Battle of Wagram', 1809, 'On 5-6 July 1809 Napoleon defeated Archduke Charles''s Austrian army at Wagram, near Vienna, in a very costly victory that involved well over 300,000 men. A massed artillery barrage helped to break the Austrian centre. The battle ended the War of the Fifth Coalition and forced Austria to make peace, but its heavy casualties showed that Napoleon''s victories were becoming more expensive.'),
  ('Mexican independence', 1810, 'On 16 September 1810 the priest Miguel Hidalgo called the people of Dolores to revolt against Spanish rule in the ''Grito de Dolores'', beginning Mexico''s War of Independence. Hidalgo was captured and executed in 1811, but others such as Morelos continued, and independence was finally achieved in 1821. Mexicans still celebrate 16 September as Independence Day.'),
  ('Battle of Borodino', 1812, 'On 7 September 1812 Napoleon''s Grande Armée fought Kutuzov''s Russian army at Borodino, about 120 km west of Moscow, in the bloodiest single day of the Napoleonic Wars, with some 70,000 casualties in all. The French took the main positions but failed to destroy the Russian army, which withdrew in good order. Napoleon entered Moscow a week later and found it largely abandoned, and fires soon destroyed much of the city. With no peace offer coming, the disastrous retreat that followed destroyed his army.'),
  ('Battle of Queenston Heights', 1812, 'On 13 October 1812 a British and Canadian force, with Mohawk allies, defeated an American invasion across the Niagara River at Queenston Heights, in the first major battle of the War of 1812. The British commander in Upper Canada, General Isaac Brock, was killed leading a counterattack on the heights and became a Canadian hero. Most of the New York militia refused to cross into Canada, which left the Americans who had crossed without support, and about 900 of them had to surrender. The victory raised Canadian morale and helped to convince many that Canada could be defended.'),
  ('Battle of Dresden', 1813, 'On 26-27 August 1813 Napoleon defeated a much larger Allied army outside Dresden in Saxony, in what was his last major victory on German soil. One of the Allied commanders, the French exile General Moreau, was mortally wounded by a cannonball. The success was soon undone, because one of his corps was destroyed at Kulm a few days later, and in October the Allies defeated him at Leipzig.'),
  ('Battle of Leipzig', 1813, 'The Battle of Leipzig, fought on 16-19 October 1813, brought together more than half a million soldiers from Austria, Russia, Prussia and Sweden against Napoleon''s army, in the largest battle in Europe before the First World War. Napoleon was forced to retreat, losing some 70,000 men killed, wounded or captured, and his German allies deserted him, as the Saxon troops changed sides in the middle of the battle. The defeat ended French control of Germany and carried the war into France, and it led to Napoleon''s abdication in 1814.'),
  ('Battle of Fort Erie', 1814, 'From August to September 1814 a British army under General Gordon Drummond besieged the American-held Fort Erie on the Niagara River, in some of the bloodiest fighting of the War of 1812. A British night assault on 15 August was beaten back with heavy losses, and an explosion in one of the bastions killed many more. On 17 September the Americans made a sortie that destroyed the British siege batteries, and the British withdrew a few days later. The Americans themselves blew up the fort and left in November, but the stand showed that their army could now hold its own against British regulars.'),
  ('Congress of Vienna', 1814, 'From September 1814 to June 1815 the Congress of Vienna, led by Austria''s Prince Metternich, brought together representatives of all the main European powers to rebuild Europe after Napoleon. It restored the Bourbon monarchy in France, redrew borders to balance the powers so that none could dominate the others, and set up a German Confederation. Its Final Act was signed on 9 June 1815, nine days before Waterloo. The settlement helped keep the great powers out of a Europe-wide war for about a century, though it also suppressed liberal and national movements.'),
  ('Battle of Waterloo', 1815, 'On 18 June 1815 Napoleon, back from exile on Elba for the Hundred Days, was defeated near Waterloo, in present-day Belgium, by Wellington''s British-led army and Blücher''s Prussians. The French cavalry charges failed to break Wellington''s squares, and the arrival of the Prussians in the evening decided the battle when the Imperial Guard''s final attack was repulsed. Napoleon abdicated four days later and was sent to St Helena, where he died in 1821. Waterloo ended more than two decades of war in Europe and gave its name to any final defeat.'),
  ('Greek War of Independence', 1821, 'The Greek War of Independence began in March 1821, when Greeks rose against Ottoman rule in the Peloponnese and the Danubian principalities. It lasted until 1829 and was marked by massacres on both sides, such as those at Tripolitsa and Chios, and by the support of philhellenes, among them the poet Lord Byron, who died at Missolonghi in 1824. The intervention of Britain, France and Russia, and the destruction of the Ottoman fleet at Navarino in 1827, secured the result. Greece became the first European nation to win independence from the Ottoman Empire, and the great powers recognized it in 1830.'),
  ('Brazilian independence', 1822, 'On 7 September 1822 Prince Pedro, the heir to the Portuguese crown who was ruling Brazil as regent, declared Brazil independent from Portugal by the Ipiranga stream, with the cry ''Independence or death!''. The Portuguese parliament had been trying to reduce Brazil to a colony again after it had been a kingdom since 1815. Pedro was crowned Emperor Pedro I in December, and after some fighting Portugal recognized the new empire in 1825. Unlike most of Spanish America, Brazil stayed united under a monarchy, which lasted until 1889.'),
  ('Monroe Doctrine', 1823, 'In his annual message to Congress on 2 December 1823, President James Monroe warned the European powers not to establish new colonies or interfere in the newly independent states of the Americas. In return the United States promised to stay out of existing European colonies and European wars. At the time the young republic had little power to enforce it, and the British navy, which also opposed Spanish reconquest, was what really protected the new Latin American states. Shaped largely by Secretary of State John Quincy Adams, the doctrine became a pillar of U.S. foreign policy and was invoked for generations afterwards.'),
  ('Bolivian independence', 1825, 'On 6 August 1825 a congress meeting at Chuquisaca declared the independence of Upper Peru, which took the name Bolivia in honour of Simón Bolívar. The Spanish had already lost the war in South America at Ayacucho in December 1824, and the last royalist force in Upper Peru ended its resistance in April 1825. Bolívar wrote the first constitution, and his lieutenant Antonio José de Sucre led the new government, and the capital, Sucre, is named after him. Bolivia later lost much territory to its neighbours, including its coastline to Chile in the War of the Pacific.'),
  ('Battle of Navarino', 1827, 'On 20 October 1827 a combined British, French and Russian fleet under Admiral Codrington destroyed most of the Ottoman and Egyptian fleet in Navarino Bay, on the Greek coast, in a battle that started by accident. The allies lost no ship, and the sultan''s forces lost thousands of men. It was the last major sea battle fought entirely between sailing ships, and it ended Ottoman hopes of crushing the Greek revolt. Greek independence followed within a few years.'),
  ('Battle of Konya', 1832, 'On 21 December 1832 Ibrahim Pasha, the son of Egypt''s ruler Muhammad Ali, defeated the Ottoman army of Grand Vizier Reşid Mehmed Pasha at Konya in Anatolia and captured the Grand Vizier himself. The road to Constantinople lay open, and Sultan Mahmud II turned for help to Russia, which sent troops to the Bosporus. In the peace of 1833 Egypt received Syria, and Russia gained a treaty of alliance that made it the Sultan''s protector. The battle showed how weak the Ottoman state had become and brought the ''Eastern Question'' to the fore.'),
  ('Texas Revolution', 1835, 'The Texas Revolution lasted from October 1835 to April 1836, when American settlers and Tejanos rose against the centralist government of Mexico and its President, Santa Anna. Texas declared independence on 2 March 1836, and Santa Anna''s army killed the defenders of the Alamo on 6 March and executed some 340 prisoners at Goliad. Sam Houston''s army surprised and routed Santa Anna''s at San Jacinto on 21 April and captured him the following day, and ''Remember the Alamo!'' became the rallying cry. The Republic of Texas joined the United States in 1845, a step that led to the Mexican-American War.'),
  ('Battle of Nezib', 1839, 'On 24 June 1839 Ibrahim Pasha''s Egyptian army routed the Ottoman army of Hafiz Pasha at Nezib, in southern Anatolia, in the second war between Egypt and the Ottoman sultan. The Ottoman army had Prussian advisers, among them the young Helmuth von Moltke, whose advice to avoid battle was ignored. Within days Sultan Mahmud II died, and the Ottoman fleet defected to Egypt. The great powers then intervened in 1840 and forced Muhammad Ali to give up Syria and return the fleet.'),
  ('Opium War begins', 1839, 'In 1839 the Qing commissioner Lin Zexu seized and destroyed more than 20,000 chests of opium belonging to British merchants at Canton, to stop the drug trade that was draining China of silver and ruining the health of its people. Britain sent a naval expedition in 1840, and its steam-powered gunboats and modern guns overwhelmed the Chinese defenders in the First Opium War. The war ended with the Treaty of Nanking in 1842 and began what the Chinese call the ''century of humiliation'', in which Western powers forced China to open its ports and give up territory and sovereign rights.'),
  ('Treaty of Nanking', 1842, 'The Treaty of Nanking, signed on 29 August 1842 on board HMS Cornwallis, ended the First Opium War. China ceded Hong Kong Island to Britain, opened five ports (Canton, Amoy, Foochow, Ningpo and Shanghai) to British trade and paid a huge indemnity of 21 million silver dollars. It was the first of the ''unequal treaties'' that Western powers forced on the Qing, and Hong Kong remained British until 1997.'),
  ('Battle of Monterrey', 1846, 'From 21 to 24 September 1846 General Zachary Taylor''s U.S. Army captured the fortified city of Monterrey from General Pedro de Ampudia''s Mexican army after three days of hard street fighting, in which the attackers lost heavily. Ampudia surrendered in return for an eight-week armistice, a lenient term that angered President Polk in Washington. The capture gave the U.S. control of north-eastern Mexico, and Taylor''s later victory at Buena Vista made him a national hero and carried him to the presidency.'),
  ('Battle of Palo Alto', 1846, 'On 8 May 1846 Zachary Taylor''s U.S. army of about 2,300 men fought General Mariano Arista''s army of about 3,700 near the Rio Grande in Texas, in the first major battle of the Mexican-American War. The U.S. ''flying artillery'', light cannon that could move and fire quickly, inflicted heavy casualties on the Mexican lines, while the Mexican guns were slower and less effective. Arista retreated that night, and the Americans won the war''s second battle at Resaca de la Palma the next day. The fighting prompted Congress to declare war a few days later.'),
  ('Battle of Resaca de la Palma', 1846, 'On 9 May 1846, the day after Palo Alto, Taylor''s army attacked General Arista''s Mexican army, which had taken position in a dry riverbed, a resaca, near the Rio Grande, and routed it in hard hand-to-hand fighting. A cavalry charge by Captain Charles May''s dragoons broke the Mexican artillery line. The defeated Mexicans fled across the river, with many drowning, and no Mexican force was left on the Texas side. Together with Palo Alto, it opened the war and was followed by a declaration of war by the U.S. Congress on 13 May.'),
  ('Battle of San Pasqual', 1846, 'On 6 December 1846 General Stephen Kearny''s tired and poorly equipped U.S. dragoons, who had marched from New Mexico, met Andrés Pico''s Californio lancers at San Pasqual, near San Diego, in a bloody dawn fight. About twenty Americans were killed, the heaviest U.S. loss in California, and Kearny himself was wounded, while the Californios lost very few. Both sides claimed victory, but with help from Kit Carson and Navy reinforcements Kearny''s force reached San Diego, and in January 1847 California was in American hands. The battle was the Californios'' most notable victory of the war.'),
  ('U.S.-Mexico War begins', 1846, 'The Mexican-American War began in 1846, after the United States annexed Texas in 1845 and the two countries disputed where its border lay. Clashes on the Rio Grande in April were followed by a U.S. declaration of war on 13 May. The U.S. army took northern Mexico, California and finally Mexico City, and the Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo in 1848 gave the United States about half of Mexico''s territory, including California and most of the present Southwest. The question of whether slavery would spread into these lands helped push the country toward the Civil War.'),
  ('Battle of Mexico City', 1847, 'From 8 to 15 September 1847 General Winfield Scott''s U.S. army fought its way into Mexico City through the battles of Molino del Rey and Chapultepec, the hilltop castle that held the Military College. Six young Mexican cadets, the Niños Héroes, are remembered for dying in its defence and are national heroes. Santa Anna''s army withdrew, and the American flag was raised over the National Palace. The fall of the capital ended the main fighting and led to the Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo in 1848.'),
  ('Battle of Sacramento', 1847, 'On 28 February 1847 Colonel Alexander Doniphan''s Missouri volunteers, fewer than 1,000 men, defeated a Mexican army of about 3,000 on the Sacramento River, north of Chihuahua. Doniphan''s artillery and his men''s rifle fire broke the Mexican position, and the Americans lost only a handful of men. His regiment had marched some 3,000 miles, from Missouri through New Mexico and across the desert, one of the longest marches in U.S. military history. The victory opened the city of Chihuahua to the Americans.'),
  ('California Gold Rush', 1848, 'On 24 January 1848 James Marshall found gold in the millrace of John Sutter''s sawmill at Coloma, California, only days before California was ceded to the United States. News of it brought some 300,000 people from the rest of the United States and from around the world, the ''forty-niners'', and San Francisco grew from a small village into a city. California became a state in 1850, but the rush devastated its Native peoples and polluted rivers and land. It was one of the biggest mass migrations in American history.'),
  ('Revolutions of 1848', 1848, 'The revolutions of 1848, the ''Springtime of the Peoples'', began in Sicily in January and spread to France, where King Louis-Philippe was overthrown, and then to Vienna, Berlin, Budapest, Prague and the Italian states. Liberals demanded constitutions, workers demanded better conditions, and national groups demanded self-rule. By 1849 most had been crushed by the old rulers, and the Frankfurt Parliament''s plan for a united Germany failed. Yet serfdom ended in the Habsburg lands, several states gained constitutions, and the national ideas of 1848 later shaped the unification of Italy and Germany.'),
  ('Compromise of 1850', 1850, 'The Compromise of 1850 was a set of five laws passed by Congress in September 1850, mainly through the work of Henry Clay and Stephen Douglas, to settle the dispute over slavery in the lands taken from Mexico. California entered the Union as a free state, Utah and New Mexico were left to decide on slavery for themselves, the slave trade was ended in Washington, D.C., and a harsher Fugitive Slave Act was adopted. The compromise postponed the Civil War by about a decade, but the Fugitive Slave Act, which obliged Northerners to help return escaped slaves, angered the North and strengthened the abolitionist movement.'),
  ('Taiping Rebellion', 1850, 'The Taiping Rebellion began in 1850 in southern China, led by Hong Xiuquan, who believed that he was the younger brother of Jesus. His Taiping Heavenly Kingdom, with its capital at Nanjing from 1853, gained millions of followers by promising land reform and equality, and it controlled much of the Yangtze valley. It was crushed in 1864, mostly by provincial armies led by Zeng Guofan and Li Hongzhang. With an estimated 20 million or more dead, it was one of the deadliest wars in history and left the Qing dynasty much weaker.'),
  ('Kansas-Nebraska Act', 1854, 'The Kansas-Nebraska Act of 1854, drafted by Senator Stephen Douglas, created the territories of Kansas and Nebraska and let their settlers vote on whether to allow slavery. This repealed the Missouri Compromise of 1820, which had banned slavery in that region, and it caused outrage in the North. Pro-slavery and anti-slavery settlers poured into Kansas and fought a small guerrilla war known as ''Bleeding Kansas''. The act destroyed the Whig Party, led to the founding of the Republican Party and brought Abraham Lincoln back into politics.'),
  ('Battle of Kars', 1855, 'In 1855, during the Crimean War, a Russian army under General Muravyov besieged the Ottoman fortress of Kars in eastern Anatolia, defended by Ottoman troops under the British officer General William Fenwick Williams, known as Williams Pasha. The defenders repulsed a major Russian assault on 29 September with heavy Russian losses, but food ran out and the starving garrison surrendered on 28 November. The Russians treated the defenders with respect, and Williams was made a baronet as ''Williams of Kars''. Kars was returned to the Ottomans in the 1856 peace, in exchange for Sevastopol.'),
  ('Mexican Reform War', 1857, 'The Reform War, or Three Years'' War, was fought in Mexico from December 1857 to January 1861 between the Liberals, led by Benito Juárez, and the Conservatives, who backed the Church and the army. It began when the Conservatives rejected the Liberal Constitution of 1857, which curtailed the privileges of the Church and the military. The Liberals, who held Veracruz, issued the Reform Laws, separating church and state and nationalizing Church property, and won in the end. The war left Mexico bankrupt, and its debts gave France a pretext to intervene in 1861 and install Maximilian as emperor.'),
  ('Battle of Solferino', 1859, 'On 24 June 1859 the French army under Napoleon III and the Piedmontese army of Victor Emmanuel II defeated the Austrians under Emperor Franz Joseph at Solferino, in northern Italy, in the biggest battle in Europe since Leipzig in 1813. About 40,000 men were killed or wounded in a single day, and many of the wounded were left without help. The Swiss businessman Henry Dunant, who saw the aftermath, wrote a book that led to the founding of the Red Cross in 1863. Austria lost Lombardy, and the victory was a major step toward Italian unification.'),
  ('Italian unification', 1859, 'In 1859 the Kingdom of Sardinia, led by Count Cavour and backed by Napoleon III''s France, defeated Austria in the Second Italian War of Independence and gained Lombardy, starting the decisive phase of Italian unification. In 1860 Giuseppe Garibaldi''s thousand volunteers conquered Sicily and southern Italy, and in March 1861 Victor Emmanuel II was proclaimed King of Italy. Venice was added in 1866 and Rome in 1870, which completed the unification. The Risorgimento joined a land that had been divided into many separate states for centuries, and it made Italy a modern nation-state.'),
  ('American Civil War', 1861, 'The American Civil War began on 12 April 1861, when Confederate guns fired on Fort Sumter, after eleven Southern states had left the Union to protect slavery following Abraham Lincoln''s election. It lasted until 1865 and killed an estimated 620,000 to 750,000 soldiers, more than any other American war. The Union victory preserved the United States, and the war ended slavery, through the Emancipation Proclamation and the Thirteenth Amendment. It also ended the South''s old plantation order and strengthened the power of the federal government.'),
  ('Battle of Antietam', 1862, 'On 17 September 1862 Robert E. Lee''s Confederate army, in its first invasion of the North, was stopped by McClellan''s Union Army of the Potomac at Antietam Creek, near Sharpsburg, Maryland, in the bloodiest single day in American history, with about 23,000 casualties. McClellan did not use his larger army to destroy Lee, who withdrew across the Potomac. Lincoln used the Union success to issue the preliminary Emancipation Proclamation five days later, which made ending slavery a goal of the war. The battle also discouraged Britain and France from recognizing the Confederacy.'),
  ('Battle of Fredericksburg', 1862, 'On 13 December 1862 General Ambrose Burnside sent his Union army across the Rappahannock River and into repeated frontal attacks on the Confederate positions at Fredericksburg, Virginia, especially Marye''s Heights, where Lee''s infantry fired from behind a stone wall. The attacks failed with terrible losses, and the Union casualties of about 12,500 were more than twice those of the Confederates. It was one of Lee''s most one-sided victories, and Union morale sank so low that Burnside was replaced by Joseph Hooker in January 1863.'),
  ('Battle of Puebla', 1862, 'On 5 May 1862 a smaller, poorly equipped Mexican army under General Ignacio Zaragoza beat back a French attack on Puebla, defending the forts of Loreto and Guadalupe against the French army, then regarded as the best in the world. The French, who had invaded Mexico to collect debts and set up a monarchy under Napoleon III, lost several hundred men and withdrew. The victory did not stop the invasion, since the French returned with reinforcements and took Puebla and Mexico City in 1863, but it became a symbol of Mexican national pride. Mexicans still celebrate it as Cinco de Mayo.'),
  ('Battle of Shiloh', 1862, 'On 6 April 1862 Confederate forces under Albert Sidney Johnston and Beauregard launched a surprise attack on Ulysses Grant''s army camped at Pittsburg Landing, Tennessee, near a church called Shiloh. Johnston, the Confederacy''s top commander in the West, was killed on the first day, and Union reinforcements arriving overnight allowed Grant to counterattack and win on 7 April. With about 23,000 casualties, the battle cost more lives than all of America''s earlier wars combined. It ended hopes of a short war and showed both sides what the conflict would cost.'),
  ('Battle of Stones River', 1862, 'From 31 December 1862 to 2 January 1863 William Rosecrans''s Union army and Braxton Bragg''s Confederate army fought at Stones River, near Murfreesboro, Tennessee, in a battle that cost about a third of the men engaged on both sides, the highest share of casualties of any major battle of the war. Bragg''s surprise attack drove back the Union right at first, but Rosecrans held his line and Bragg withdrew. The Union victory came after the defeat at Fredericksburg and lifted Northern morale, and Lincoln told Rosecrans that a defeat there might have been more than the nation could bear.'),
  ('Battle of Chancellorsville', 1863, 'From 30 April to 6 May 1863 Robert E. Lee, with about 60,000 men, defeated Joseph Hooker''s Union army of about 130,000 near Chancellorsville, Virginia. Lee boldly divided his army in front of the larger enemy, and on 2 May Stonewall Jackson''s corps marched around the Union flank and routed the Union right. It is often called Lee''s perfect battle, but Jackson was accidentally shot by his own men that night and died eight days later, a loss Lee could not replace. The victory encouraged Lee to invade the North, which led to Gettysburg.'),
  ('Battle of Gettysburg', 1863, 'From 1 to 3 July 1863 Lee''s Army of Northern Virginia, in its second invasion of the North, was defeated at Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, by George Meade''s Army of the Potomac. On the third day Pickett''s Charge, a massive assault across open ground at the centre of the Union line, was shot to pieces. It was the bloodiest battle of the war, with about 50,000 casualties, and the Confederacy never again invaded the North in force. In November Lincoln gave the Gettysburg Address at the dedication of the soldiers'' cemetery.'),
  ('Battle of Vicksburg', 1863, 'From 18 May to 4 July 1863 Ulysses Grant''s Union army besieged Vicksburg, Mississippi, the Confederate fortress on the bluffs over the Mississippi River, and starved its garrison of about 29,000 men under General Pemberton into surrender. Grant had won his way to the city with a bold campaign in which he cut loose from his supply lines and defeated the Confederate forces in a series of battles. The fall of Vicksburg, a day after Gettysburg ended, was a turning point of the war, as it gave the Union control of the whole Mississippi and split the Confederacy in two. It also made Grant the Union''s leading general.'),
  ('Emancipation Proclamation', 1863, 'On 1 January 1863 President Abraham Lincoln issued the Emancipation Proclamation, declaring free all enslaved people in the areas still in rebellion, more than three million people. It did not apply to the border states or to Union-held areas, so freedom came in practice only as the Union army advanced. Yet it turned the war into a fight against slavery, opened the army to Black soldiers, about 180,000 of whom served, and made it much harder for Britain or France to side with the Confederacy. The Thirteenth Amendment of 1865 abolished slavery everywhere in the United States.'),
  ('Battle of Mansfield', 1864, 'On 8 April 1864 the Confederate General Richard Taylor, with a smaller force, attacked and routed the Union vanguard of Nathaniel Banks''s army at Mansfield, in Louisiana, during the Red River Campaign. The Union army had been marching up the Red River to capture Shreveport and the cotton of the region, and Banks''s column was strung out along a narrow road. The attack drove the Union troops back in disorder, and Banks retreated, so the campaign ended in failure. It was one of the last big Confederate victories and a clear Union setback west of the Mississippi.'),
  ('Battle of Petersburg', 1864, 'The Battle of Petersburg began on 15 June 1864, when Ulysses Grant''s army tried to seize Petersburg, Virginia, the railway junction that supplied Richmond and Lee''s army, before Lee''s main force arrived. Poorly coordinated Union attacks and a small Confederate force that held on gave Lee time to reinforce the city, and the assault became a siege. For nine months the two armies fought from long lines of trenches, including the Union mine and failed attack at the Crater, at a cost of some 70,000 casualties. Lee abandoned the city on 2 April 1865 and surrendered at Appomattox a week later.'),
  ('Battle of the Wilderness', 1864, 'On 5-7 May 1864 Ulysses Grant''s Army of the Potomac met Lee''s army in the Wilderness, a tangled forest in Virginia, at the start of the Overland Campaign. The dense woods made it hard to see or move, artillery was almost useless, and fires in the underbrush burned many of the wounded to death. The battle ended without a clear winner and cost about 28,000 casualties, but unlike earlier Union commanders Grant did not retreat, and he moved south to keep up the pressure. That began a relentless campaign of attrition that led to Lee''s surrender the following year.'),
  ('U.S. Reconstruction begins', 1865, 'After the Confederacy surrendered in April 1865, the United States began Reconstruction, the effort to bring the defeated Southern states back into the Union and to define the rights of four million freed people. Congress passed the Thirteenth, Fourteenth and Fifteenth Amendments, which abolished slavery and guaranteed citizenship and the vote to Black men, and the Freedmen''s Bureau helped them with schools and work. Southern whites resisted, often with violence by groups like the Ku Klux Klan. Reconstruction ended in 1877, when federal troops were withdrawn, and Southern states then built the Jim Crow system of segregation.'),
  ('Battle of Königgrätz', 1866, 'On 3 July 1866 the Prussian armies under Helmuth von Moltke decisively defeated the Austrians at Königgrätz (Sadová) in Bohemia, in one of the largest battles in Europe before the First World War. Prussian soldiers with breech-loading needle guns fired much faster than the Austrians'' muzzle-loaders, and railways and the telegraph brought the Prussian armies together on the field. The war was over in about seven weeks, and Austria was excluded from German affairs. Prussia took the lead in Germany, and this paved the way for the German Empire in 1871.'),
  ('Purchase of Alaska', 1867, 'In 1867 the United States bought Alaska, then called Russian America, from Russia for $7.2 million, about two cents an acre. Secretary of State William Seward negotiated the deal, and Russia, short of money after the Crimean War, thought the remote colony hard to defend against Britain. Many Americans mocked it as ''Seward''s Folly'', but the gold rushes of the 1890s and the discovery of oil at Prudhoe Bay in 1968 showed its value. Alaska became the 49th state in 1959.'),
  ('Japanese Meiji Restoration', 1868, 'In 1868 the Tokugawa shogunate, which had ruled Japan for more than 250 years, was overthrown, and imperial rule was restored under the young Emperor Meiji. The new leaders abolished the feudal domains and the privileges of the samurai, created a modern army with conscription, schools, railways and industries, and adopted a constitution in 1889. Within a generation Japan changed from an isolated feudal country into an industrial power. It defeated China in 1895 and Russia in 1905, and became the first non-Western great power of the modern age.'),
  ('Suez Canal opens', 1869, 'The Suez Canal opened on 17 November 1869 after ten years of work, linking the Mediterranean with the Red Sea across Egypt. The French diplomat Ferdinand de Lesseps led the project, which was built by tens of thousands of Egyptian labourers, many of whom died. It cut the voyage from Europe to India and East Asia by thousands of kilometres by avoiding the long trip around Africa, and it made Egypt strategically vital. Britain bought Egypt''s shares in 1875 and occupied the country in 1882, and Egypt''s nationalization of the canal in 1956 led to the Suez Crisis.'),
  ('Battle of Sedan', 1870, 'On 1-2 September 1870 the German armies under Moltke surrounded the French Army of Châlons at Sedan, near the Belgian border, and shelled it into surrender. Emperor Napoleon III, who was with the army, was captured together with more than 100,000 soldiers. The news brought down the Second Empire in Paris, where a republic was proclaimed on 4 September, but the war went on for five more months. The decisive German victory led to the siege of Paris and to the creation of the German Empire.'),
  ('Battle of Le Mans', 1871, 'On 10-12 January 1871 a German army under Prince Frederick Charles defeated General Chanzy''s Second Army of the Loire at Le Mans, in western France, during the last winter of the Franco-Prussian War. The French, many of them raw conscripts, fought in snow and bitter cold and were beaten back with heavy losses. It ended organised French resistance in the west of the country, and Chanzy withdrew his men towards Laval. With Paris under siege and the other armies defeated, France signed an armistice at the end of January.'),
  ('German unification', 1871, 'On 18 January 1871, in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles, King Wilhelm I of Prussia was proclaimed German Emperor, creating the German Empire. Otto von Bismarck had united the German states under Prussian leadership ''by blood and iron'', through short wars against Denmark in 1864, Austria in 1866 and France in 1870-71. The new empire at once became the strongest power on the European continent, upsetting the old balance among the great powers. France''s loss of Alsace and part of Lorraine and its humiliation at Versailles fed a bitterness that helped bring on the First World War.'),
  ('Paris Commune', 1871, 'The Paris Commune was a revolutionary government that ruled the French capital from 18 March to 28 May 1871, after the defeat in the Franco-Prussian War and the siege of Paris. Working-class National Guardsmen and radicals rebelled against the national government at Versailles, and the Commune passed measures such as the separation of church and state and a ban on night work in bakeries. The army retook the city in ''Bloody Week'', killing thousands of Communards, and thousands more were imprisoned or deported. Karl Marx praised it, and it became a model and a warning for later socialist and communist movements.'),
  ('Ottoman constitutional era', 1876, 'On 23 December 1876 Sultan Abdulhamid II proclaimed the first Ottoman constitution, drafted by reformers led by the statesman Midhat Pasha, making the empire a constitutional monarchy with an elected parliament. It was meant to answer European demands for reform and to head off foreign intervention in the Balkans. Less than two years later, in February 1878, the Sultan suspended parliament, using the war with Russia as an excuse, and he ruled as an autocrat for thirty years. The constitution was restored by the Young Turk Revolution of 1908.'),
  ('Battle of Plevna', 1877, 'From July to December 1877 the Ottoman general Osman Pasha held the fortified town of Plevna (now Pleven), in northern Bulgaria, against much larger Russian and Romanian armies. Three major assaults on the town were beaten back with heavy losses, and the Russians finally surrounded it and starved it into surrender. Osman broke out on 10 December but was wounded and had to give up, with some 40,000 men. Plevna delayed the Russian advance by months, but its fall opened the way across the Balkan mountains and led to a peace that freed Bulgaria from direct Ottoman rule.'),
  ('Battle of San Stefano', 1878, 'San Stefano, today''s Yeşilköy, is a village on the Sea of Marmara just outside Constantinople, where the Russian army halted in early 1878 after its victories over the Ottomans in the Russo-Turkish War. The treaty signed there on 3 March 1878 created a large autonomous Bulgaria, recognized the independence of Serbia, Montenegro and Romania, and gave Russia lands in the Caucasus. Britain and Austria-Hungary feared Russian influence in the Balkans, and the great powers forced a revision at the Congress of Berlin the same year. The ''Greater Bulgaria'' of San Stefano remained a goal of Bulgarian nationalists for decades.'),
  ('Battle of Rorke''s Drift', 1879, 'On 22-23 January 1879 about 150 British soldiers under Lieutenants John Chard and Gonville Bromhead defended the mission station and supply depot at Rorke''s Drift, in Natal, against some 3,000-4,000 Zulu warriors. The attack came on the same day that a British column had been destroyed at Isandlwana, and the defenders built barricades from mealie bags and biscuit boxes and fought hand to hand all night, until the Zulus withdrew at dawn. Eleven Victoria Crosses were awarded for the action, the most for a single battle. It gave Britain a heroic story to balance the disaster at Isandlwana, and the 1964 film Zulu made it world-famous.'),
  ('Berlin Conference', 1884, 'At the Berlin Conference of 1884-1885, called by Otto von Bismarck, fourteen countries agreed on rules for dividing up Africa, and no African representatives were present. The powers agreed that a claim to a territory had to be backed by ''effective occupation'', recognized King Leopold II''s personal rule over the Congo, and guaranteed free trade on the Congo and Niger rivers. The conference sped up the ''Scramble for Africa''. The borders drawn by Europeans, often ignoring ethnic and political realities, still shape the continent today.'),
  ('Battle of Dogali', 1887, 'On 26 January 1887 a column of about 500 Italian soldiers marching to relieve a besieged fort was ambushed and almost wiped out by a much larger Ethiopian force under Ras Alula at Dogali, in what is now Eritrea. The Italians, who had been expanding inland from their port of Massawa, were shocked by the loss of more than 400 men. Rome sent bigger forces and in the end stumbled into the war that brought the Italian defeat at Adwa in 1896. The dead are remembered in Rome by the Piazza dei Cinquecento, ''Square of the Five Hundred'', near the main railway station.'),
  ('Sino-Japanese War', 1894, 'The First Sino-Japanese War of 1894-1895 was fought over influence in Korea, which China claimed as a tributary and Japan wished to control. Japan''s modernized army and navy won decisive victories at the Yalu River, Port Arthur and Weihaiwei and crushed the Qing forces. In the Treaty of Shimonoseki in 1895 China recognized Korea''s independence, ceded Taiwan and paid a large indemnity. The war showed that Meiji Japan had become Asia''s leading military power and exposed Qing weakness, and the European powers then rushed to claim concessions in China.'),
  ('Battle of Adwa', 1896, 'On 1 March 1896 an Ethiopian army of some 100,000 men under Emperor Menelik II, armed with modern rifles and artillery as well as traditional weapons, crushed an Italian colonial force of about 17,000 at Adwa, in northern Ethiopia. The Italian columns, led by General Oreste Baratieri, had lost their way in the mountains and were defeated one after the other, with thousands killed and many more captured. Italy had to recognize Ethiopia''s independence in October. It was one of the few times an African army defeated a European colonial power, and Adwa became a symbol of African resistance, while Italy''s humiliation helped fuel Mussolini''s invasion of Ethiopia in 1935.'),
  ('Battle of Manila Bay', 1898, 'On 1 May 1898 Commodore George Dewey''s American squadron steamed into Manila Bay in the Philippines overnight and destroyed the Spanish Pacific fleet under Admiral Montojo in a few hours at dawn. Every Spanish warship was sunk or captured, while no American was killed in the fighting and none of the American ships was seriously damaged. It was the first major battle of the Spanish-American War and made Dewey a national hero. The victory led to the American capture of Manila and, after the war, to the U.S. takeover of the Philippines.'),
  ('Hundred Days'' Reform', 1898, 'From 11 June to 21 September 1898 the young Guangxu Emperor, advised by the scholars Kang Youwei and Liang Qichao, issued a flood of edicts to modernize the Qing empire on the model of Meiji Japan. They created modern schools and a university in Beijing, reformed the exams and the government, and encouraged railways and industry. Conservative officials opposed it, and the Empress Dowager Cixi staged a coup, put the Emperor under house arrest and had six of the reformers executed, while Kang and Liang fled abroad. The failure showed that the Qing could not reform itself from the top and helped lead to the 1911 revolution.'),
  ('Spanish-American War', 1898, 'The Spanish-American War of 1898 grew out of Cuba''s rebellion against Spanish rule, and American anger rose after the battleship USS Maine blew up in Havana harbour in February. The United States won quickly, destroying Spanish fleets at Manila Bay and Santiago and defeating Spanish forces in Cuba, where Theodore Roosevelt''s Rough Riders became famous. In the Treaty of Paris, Spain gave up Cuba, ceded Puerto Rico and Guam and sold the Philippines to the United States for $20 million. The war ended the Spanish empire in the Americas and the Philippines and made the United States a world power with overseas possessions.'),
  ('Battle of Colenso', 1899, 'On 15 December 1899 General Redvers Buller''s British army tried to cross the Tugela River at Colenso, in Natal, on its way to relieve the besieged town of Ladysmith. The Boers under Louis Botha, hidden in trenches along the river and armed with accurate, smokeless Mauser rifles, shot down the British columns, and ten British guns were lost when the artillery went too far forward. The British suffered more than 1,100 casualties, against fewer than 40 for the Boers. Colenso was the third British defeat of ''Black Week'', and the shock led to Buller''s replacement as commander-in-chief by Lord Roberts.'),
  ('Boxer Uprising', 1899, 'The Boxer Uprising began in 1899 in northern China, where a secret society called the Righteous and Harmonious Fists, nicknamed the Boxers by foreigners, attacked foreigners, missionaries and Chinese Christians, angered by foreign influence and the spread of Christianity. In 1900 the Qing court under the Empress Dowager Cixi backed them, and they besieged the foreign legations in Beijing for 55 days. An international force of eight nations relieved the city in August 1900. The Boxer Protocol of 1901 forced China to pay a huge indemnity and allow foreign troops in Beijing, and it left the Qing dynasty even weaker.');
